#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197971 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "fe87a57b-09fc-5c98-9d4c-e3ba3b95c2a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12419" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/263853df5f080da778a34caacea945119099326f0ba27806d851f793416e9f92.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 0, \\, x, \\, -z^2 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nDetermine the missing expression." :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,x,-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = 0, \\qquad\nQ = x, \\qquad\nR = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "5fb5ffe1-51cb-5db8-b0a4-effd27cc623f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12420" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\, y, \\, 0 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinder $z=1-x^2$ and the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y, \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (-2x)-y\\cdot (0)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y , \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (2x)-y\\cdot (2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d30fd415-bef5-5bad-854f-586531d53c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378699} {:answer/id #uuid "8cba9584-7c2c-51c1-8955-b32bb09d86b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378700} {:answer/id #uuid "e426fb22-8e31-596a-a5a4-0b9ed2a693f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-6x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378701} {:answer/id #uuid "04b66e8b-f9a7-5730-b513-8a9797591ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378702} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1abf14-3421-564c-8532-63312b865da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb1abf14-3421-564c-8532-63312b865da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378703} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf521ea-fc63-5207-ab84-eb3a3c45490f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87242b70-612b-5db0-9c6f-2022ddd8e964" :question/math-academy-id "q-161895" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c498de6-9a5f-5702-9de8-add8ffe1b816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378706} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d5db3a-9b49-57d1-9674-7f7f712bd5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378707} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a30733-8953-5156-8058-2567c5d9c088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378708} {:answer/id #uuid "e4135d67-f437-5326-82c8-5bfabe55338c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378709} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e41be-21f7-50c0-add8-402f24c9cfc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c498de6-9a5f-5702-9de8-add8ffe1b816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378706} :answer-field/id #uuid "9423072b-912c-5bf1-beea-06b077cc7f29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3eea45a-e598-5192-b757-2e1b9a1153d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161901" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/c7f140ad2c5687db48171819e1efaa858701af881ebbee57ff835da569f30947.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨{z}^{2},0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192c4460-e346-5161-a9f9-ef6c54ae210d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378713} {:answer/id #uuid "6712318d-b9ee-5f22-a548-42e5f6083ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378714} {:answer/id #uuid "83a82167-a9b9-5ca0-b834-d0d12fe1b1f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378715} {:answer/id #uuid "08045a6e-6387-5980-b29e-b3ac005cf271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378716} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc97b4b-78dc-59ca-a5c7-dbec2ed100c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192c4460-e346-5161-a9f9-ef6c54ae210d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378713} :answer-field/id #uuid "025aa2b3-25c3-5a10-ae18-f680a03c33fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "150a753b-c416-5943-8cfa-2d89a5b17df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161904" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-(0)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0321e38e-1c77-54cb-9cbd-f9ac0526471e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378720} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe1a8c2-8abc-5140-af1e-95aff8bb495a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378721} {:answer/id #uuid "b91ee57c-77ce-51e2-b8cc-40f5fc7419c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-5x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378722} {:answer/id #uuid "6467024a-608e-543a-b155-2fc0237d33db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378723} {:answer/id #uuid "db0dea77-fa80-5800-a581-315c42c62676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0321e38e-1c77-54cb-9cbd-f9ac0526471e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378720} :answer-field/id #uuid "0946e5a6-7068-5678-be77-e55ddaa8ce9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64b8ff17-b4b5-5df6-a8c6-0e2b7a0379fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161905" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4fc4a51-e5cf-55f3-bf0b-d292d055df1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194190} {:answer/id #uuid "e06a40c6-d550-5843-953f-340f162a132f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194191} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c7d517-23f6-5ae5-a893-ce88d1568696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194192} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3045f4-4943-5d12-8b08-ae1d109cd0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194193} {:answer/id #uuid "66917aab-4a61-553e-8478-7715ad47eacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x - y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e3045f4-4943-5d12-8b08-ae1d109cd0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194193} :answer-field/id #uuid "570ebdc1-1d0c-513a-a91f-ce1b245181f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f651353-551c-55cb-9916-e19d1d757989" :question/math-academy-id "q-161907" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1+x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1+x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 2x-0\\cdot 2y+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x-1\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f36fb53-30eb-5eeb-a5cb-d2243a9a5cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378727} {:answer/id #uuid "af99e1ee-a46c-5d57-9f3d-5346f2ea9bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378728} {:answer/id #uuid "5b293e36-7131-5414-90be-87ddf25eb079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378729} {:answer/id #uuid "0613470b-ec84-546e-86ca-38676c9a8e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378730} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7f910c-2c8d-5dbe-9358-5018562e4abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0613470b-ec84-546e-86ca-38676c9a8e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378730} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce3239f-6733-58fe-b720-d388a67a1827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "883193fd-f5be-5d1c-8ffa-32b09d1431cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161908" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/2fff2eab1f5670dfcd3351dcbb9be1b3110e6ebdcd452cf4c5a273611d454c8b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eee982ac-cfca-5d03-a57c-278b2d139100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378734} {:answer/id #uuid "d15df8d0-78cb-5542-9a8e-1d2392044cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378735} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8e1246-763b-5440-86da-c1ebeb83a284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378736} {:answer/id #uuid "b17abe20-b1f2-5202-8251-02dcefc35c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378737} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f88975-d93a-54bb-893b-88f1106c897f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+(x+y)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d15df8d0-78cb-5542-9a8e-1d2392044cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378735} :answer-field/id #uuid "c36c108b-f740-54fa-805a-1ad869b6e112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39e1c3e2-6428-5a2a-8b1d-95017ac2ef6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161910" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/01779de50ae00cf2883bee0e021638a423989b87a69aaa17e0332c4a671a32ca.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,z,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b772ee87-d705-5659-b051-efc94d4c90c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378741} {:answer/id #uuid "29d7259e-0555-5c91-8a3e-b9884803f7f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2xy-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378742} {:answer/id #uuid "8ada3790-56c2-531a-abe5-5fb085bee02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+2x-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378743} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef4f59d-12ba-50a5-bbdb-0e4f79061f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2x+6y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378744} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f824a0-4b51-572c-bdd2-e6e4a8b8394c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}-12xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b772ee87-d705-5659-b051-efc94d4c90c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378741} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52623e9-5877-5e25-a2a5-c27c07ea810f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d156036-efde-59a7-b837-c977a3c50b6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161911" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/5709af87720dcb052d8ffc6cc600c5889e45849aee6bed69ec3646e43c3cefb1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,1,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3,\\,Q=1,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)\\cdot 2x-1\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba2bec9d-e1f2-5711-a943-9b3f687ebb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378748} {:answer/id #uuid "251659f2-bb76-5360-a291-a30930c23c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378749} {:answer/id #uuid "31015939-d6d7-5b08-ba18-04bc236d7cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378750} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ec492f-d185-5d74-952d-0227ebfb4d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378751} {:answer/id #uuid "6703398b-5dd0-5e2d-b55b-77476519b427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6703398b-5dd0-5e2d-b55b-77476519b427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378752} :answer-field/id #uuid "15efbc2f-4a60-5878-b4a6-cb1313a0bf80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b5db020-d92f-50c2-8c6b-3f44b9d6b975" :question/math-academy-id "q-161913" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $z={y}^{2}-2.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={y}^{2}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-2)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}={({y}^{2}-2)}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-{({y}^{2}-2)}^{2}\\cdot (0)+0\\cdot (2y)+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80d7b34b-9462-548f-bf17-08292ef47d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{y}^{3}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378755} {:answer/id #uuid "d69f35c9-8b2f-52b5-98d4-a2d94aacc39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378756} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c826f0-9a32-5c2b-9a29-0e956dd2ce77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y{x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378757} {:answer/id #uuid "918a6ff8-3a90-53dc-b1fe-a8d9cf507b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378758} {:answer/id #uuid "8876fb0a-3f4c-5478-9932-e5456974cca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8876fb0a-3f4c-5478-9932-e5456974cca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378759} :answer-field/id #uuid "9962cdec-7208-5071-8ab5-9181c1a293ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91499b2a-f503-536e-9f6f-5cde9bd57186" :question/math-academy-id "q-161914" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0+y+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (2x)-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(0-2y{x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
